## output

When Tola moved to a new school, everything felt unfamiliar—the classrooms, the faces, even the way the bell sounded. She kept her head down and counted the days until things might feel normal.

On her third day, she dropped her books in the hallway. Papers scattered everywhere. Before she could panic, someone knelt beside her.

“Hi, I’m Zainab,” the girl said with a warm smile. “These floors are always hungry for homework.”

Tola laughed despite herself. Together, they gathered the papers, and that small moment turned into sitting together at lunch. Lunch turned into studying after school. Studying turned into sharing secrets under the mango tree near the playground.

Zainab had a way of making hard days lighter. When Tola missed her old home, Zainab listened. When Zainab struggled with math, Tola explained it patiently. They balanced each other—quiet and bold, careful and adventurous.

One afternoon, during the school talent show, Tola froze backstage. She had signed up to read a poem but suddenly felt too nervous to step forward.

Zainab squeezed her hand. “I’ll stand in the front row,” she said. “Just look at me.”

Tola walked onto the stage. The lights were bright, but in the crowd, she found Zainab’s steady smile. Her voice stopped shaking. The words flowed.

Afterward, they hugged, laughing with relief.

That day, Tola realized friendship isn’t just about sharing good times. It’s about having someone who stands with you when you’re scared, who believes in you until you believe in yourself.

And sometimes, that makes all the difference.

- overcoming fear

The first time Daniel stood at the edge of the diving board, his legs refused to move. The pool below looked deeper than the ocean, darker than night. The laughter of other swimmers echoed behind him, but all he could hear was his heartbeat.

He had climbed the ladder three times already that afternoon—each time walking back down.

“You don’t have to jump,” his sister said gently from the water. “But you can.”

Daniel stared at the surface below. He wasn’t afraid of swimming. He was afraid of the moment in between—the fall, the loss of control.

He closed his eyes and imagined the worst that could happen. A splash. A gulp of water. Embarrassment.

Then he imagined the best. Pride. Relief. Freedom.

Taking a slow breath, he counted. “Three… two… one.”

Before his fear could argue, he bent his knees and pushed off.

For a second, the world disappeared. Wind rushed past his ears. His stomach flipped. Then—

Splash!

Cool water surrounded him. He kicked upward and broke the surface, gasping.

He was laughing.

The board didn’t look so high anymore. The fear hadn’t vanished—it had simply been smaller than his courage.

As he swam to the side, Daniel realized something important: fear grows when you face away from it. But when you step forward, even shaking, it begins to shrink.

And sometimes, all it takes is one brave jump.


